gned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(5,5).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50d89868-a75f-579e-a975-aee85bb121db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50d89868-a75f-579e-a975-aee85bb121db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564612} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6850feb-e59a-57fa-8eb3-b345830389df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564630} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fae41de2-30dc-52c2-b767-4cd2fca0ebfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fae41de2-30dc-52c2-b767-4cd2fca0ebfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564651} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb4bb037-4458-5443-9885-b84764763734" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f379e31b-1455-531c-aca1-32e394f270e2" :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $1,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${45}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=1,\\,\\theta ={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565409 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7ee46e-dc63-58c3-89bb-875458d30291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564964} {:answer/id #uuid "360b1df4-8a51-5c90-827b-ce21bb471dc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565018} {:answer/id #uuid "500e4e0d-01de-5798-a08b-46e3ec45ecf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565051} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3a2024-7096-5374-881d-7381cde4fee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565078} {:answer/id #uuid "63379f06-acca-58df-bcc7-05308d492f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c3a2024-7096-5374-881d-7381cde4fee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565078} :answer-field/id #uuid "b673d111-8088-5a19-9579-504b507d8a88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0443135d-0cff-565f-8ee8-219a9e567bef" :question/math-academy-id "q-99351" :question/problem "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(-\\frac{9}{5{x}^{8}}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(-\\frac{9}{5{x}^{8}}\\right)=-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the graph of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}:$\n\n![](images/ae/aeeed013b57f5fd8a554ca6613bf9e3fa9419ae2e0fd5920397c5b9cb0dd88fb.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFinally, the negative factor of $-\\frac{9}{5}$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}=-\\infty .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565411 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b992071f-5ad2-5b15-8cfa-8cae80d5ae5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b992071f-5ad2-5b15-8cfa-8cae80d5ae5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565218} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f3879b6-ecd9-5bd8-a6a5-eca6fd88dfd9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0883765a-f056-556c-bee6-eafc65b5e91c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334164" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{-3}{{x}^{8}}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-3}{{x}^{8}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}\\right)=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}=-3\\cdot 0=0\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc7c654f-b8c1-596d-ae22-8f0756107b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc7c654f-b8c1-596d-ae22-8f0756107b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565323} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f15e0b-05f8-5013-b9b0-8f30387b49a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0f04e402-41a0-5006-8eb9-65c2d82f6e13" :question/math-academy-id "q-300983" :question/problem "![](images/64/6469991ee6751110f55ce6462c4c3f2e406d8b61fabcb6c2d2cc1515ec12dd2c.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $g(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}=$\n\n {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-2.$\n\nNow, rewrite the target limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${x}^{3}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}=-2\\cdot 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43429be9-bb0e-5b18-86bb-fbdf79bb94bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565036} {:answer/id #uuid "46e04dd7-a13a-5fbe-b115-b96df353ce1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565040} {:answer/id #uuid "70c09658-cf29-5193-8d12-ec120b53f9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565107} {:answer/id #uuid "9849d06c-d6fa-5f44-b558-a71dc686c4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565167} {:answer/id #uuid "c447fc46-df17-5027-b37f-7ea6e75b40a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46e04dd7-a13a-5fbe-b115-b96df353ce1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565040} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f63655d-d2a8-5b20-b3ce-1835b71b6875" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b8c4518-97e5-5ab3-a859-e015cbefbbce" :question/math-academy-id "q-36453" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{7}{3x}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits,  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{7}{3x} & =\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{x}=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31acdef2-9f74-5071-a506-a89cb8de8967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a<\\frac{33}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565012} {:answer/id #uuid "57dc35d6-a280-5f88-b102-c62b5b783067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a>\\frac{33}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565074} {:answer/id #uuid "ad14d68e-ca86-505f-bc2e-62689bb72b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a<-\\frac{33}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565196} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9847dc-820c-5f4c-ac3e-314918c2a2b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a>-\\frac{33}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565222} {:answer/id #uuid "dcee4a9e-e91f-5041-879e-d70732fa07ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a>\\frac{33}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57dc35d6-a280-5f88-b102-c62b5b783067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a>\\frac{33}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565074} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cd1d0a2-4c61-5c3b-bb28-d1ae4bccad5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a18f4a9-430a-58c6-95f9-9ffd1a99116a" :question/math-academy-id "q-154925" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3a-7>\\frac{8a+5}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-7 & >\\frac{8a+5}{4} \\\\ 4\\cdot (3a-7) & >4\\cdot \\left(\\frac{8a+5}{4}\\right) \\\\ 4\\cdot (3a-7) & >\\cancel{4}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{8a+5}{\\cancel{4}}\\right) \\\\ 4\\cdot (3a-7) & >8a+5 \\\\ 12a-28 & >8a+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12a-28 & >8a+5 \\\\ 12a-28-8a & >8a+5-8a \\\\ 4a-28 & >5 \\\\ 4a-28+28 & >5+28 \\\\ 4a & >33 \\\\ \\frac{4a}{4} & >\\frac{33}{4} \\\\ a & >\\frac{33}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a>\\frac{33}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c334b8-5f64-5024-8be8-3440026a135f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564995} {:answer/id #uuid "b3785525-f411-5114-943e-5a2cf050adfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565205} {:answer/id #uuid "b689f50d-16ad-525a-aff0-c88e838473ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565212} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cccb62-ae0c-505e-946a-32fd9dfe1c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565241} {:answer/id #uuid "d4522578-3775-5aa6-9639-2a945876ec85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7cccb62-ae0c-505e-946a-32fd9dfe1c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565241} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1873304-acce-598c-9592-a96cff0a14c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "585a757a-6bf9-546a-9bf9-80289d848f80" :question/math-academy-id "q-74968" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\\frac{0\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=-x$,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b16f81c-e174-5d24-b5c3-e147e2a28ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b16f81c-e174-5d24-b5c3-e147e2a28ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565173} :answer-field/id #uuid "617e3c87-3819-5e4d-88da-1609df57e47e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565356} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52ebf2e5-0884-5026-a89a-4586159854f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52ebf2e5-0884-5026-a89a-4586159854f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565060} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd9b0211-f69a-526f-b18b-0a11d5fc2f14" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5cd68e49-7c20-564f-9257-0043b16b7cd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-181417" :question/problem "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{x+6}{4}>\\frac{2x}{3}.$\n\nThe solution is $x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+6}{4} & >\\frac{2x}{3} \\\\ 12\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x+6}{4}\\right) & >12\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right) \\\\ 3\\cdot (x+6) & >4\\cdot 2x \\\\ 3x+18 & >8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+18 & >8x \\\\ 3x+18-8x & >8x-8x \\\\ 18-5x & >0 \\\\ 18-5x-18 & >0-18 \\\\ -5x & >-18 \\\\ \\frac{-5x}{-5} & \\,\\,<\\,\\,\\frac{-18}{-5} \\\\ x & <\\frac{18}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x<\\frac{18}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "612ea857-8d13-5f8a-bc55-dd2e8b0ba360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "612ea857-8d13-5f8a-bc55-dd2e8b0ba360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565087} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb2cc812-d3f1-5ddb-9bec-85884d6fb255" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8006767d-6578-52a2-aa78-8300e8ed36b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334193" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-4}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-4}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=-4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $({x}^{2}-3)^{3}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=-4\\cdot 0=0\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cecc548-9538-5c7d-9a8f-bea859b123f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cecc548-9538-5c7d-9a8f-bea859b123f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565026} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8c07c0a-9297-5a81-902b-be41db3eb020" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "940dd7e5-a02e-5ae8-8d31-37203bbfab5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-305776" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\left(-\\frac{9}{4(x-5)^{6}}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\left(-\\frac{9}{4(x-5)^{6}}\\right)\\,=\\,-\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\cdot \\,{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $5$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/57/574b270248f2acc2a2eb3403f5626e420f5f16abc6e9e01dcb147df83e5806b5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFinally, the negative factor of $-\\frac{9}{4}$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}=-\\infty .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c9f894b-0695-5fb5-a2fc-03e32b1ccede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565009} {:answer/id #uuid "61321b40-2ea0-5afa-9cb1-4cc94c8b9dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565088} {:answer/id #uuid "9c669607-1843-5584-8f41-4389bb11e66b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565176} {:answer/id #uuid "b380e63b-0a3b-5d84-8474-0490488b54a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565206} {:answer/id #uuid "eec60117-16db-52c1-b4e7-87394fffcc2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61321b40-2ea0-5afa-9cb1-4cc94c8b9dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565088} :answer-field/id #uuid "2235b94a-23b2-515a-9cb2-13dc47980c69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9876167b-d3d3-5f4c-9305-b455d21b66dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-342816" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-3y={u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(4t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /4}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{1}{s-3}+\\frac{s\\,{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{({s}^{2}+16)(s-3)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{3t}+\\frac{1}{25}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$ \n\nHint: You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the initial value problem, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s).$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first ignore the exponential term ${e}^{-\\pi s/4}$ (which corresponds to a time shift) and focus on the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+16)(s-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s-3)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+16),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+16)(s-3)}=\\frac{A}{s-3}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+16}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns=A({s}^{2}+16)+(Bs+C)(s-3).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n3=A({3}^{2}+16)+0\\,⟹\\,25A=3\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{3}{25}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=-\\frac{3}{25}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n0=16A-3C\\,⟹\\,0=16\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{25}\\right)-3C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{16}{25}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{25}(\\frac{3}{s-3}-3\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}+4\\cdot \\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{25}(3\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-3}\\}-3\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}\\}+4\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}\\}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}(3{e}^{3t}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/4}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}[3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $cos\\,(4\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$ and $sin\\,(4\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}(3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})(3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)={e}^{3t}+\\frac{1}{25}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8429822b-a07a-5669-ae42-aeb9f8f5a3d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{x}+10^x\\ln 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565144} {:answer/id #uuid "e043e6fb-f365-4d9f-88d3-9cccb84784f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{x}+10^{x}\\ln10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8429822b-a07a-5669-ae42-aeb9f8f5a3d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{x}+10^x\\ln 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565144} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cbfb684-4d22-5eea-b2a7-3432b18ace7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a201ee06-61a2-5773-8a24-56af686eb568" :question/math-academy-id "q-339978" :question/problem "Given that $y=-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{10}^{x}-1$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{10}^{x}-1) \\\\  & =-3\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+{10}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{x}+{10}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c20ac5a-f811-58a6-b83e-5889a9700680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c20ac5a-f811-58a6-b83e-5889a9700680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565127} :answer-field/id #uuid "0556b669-b38b-5beb-96f6-08e8bd808d5b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565328} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b40dda2b-d093-582e-8a93-141dcd009b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b40dda2b-d093-582e-8a93-141dcd009b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565209} :answer-field/id #uuid "592b7cac-318f-5d38-9731-e0a49d20cd01" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a275c58b-24cd-59ec-af04-d47c2cd4c395" :question/math-academy-id "q-181376" :question/problem "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{3-5z}{8}\\le 6-2z.$\n\nThe solution is $z$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3-5z}{8} & \\le 6-2z \\\\ 8\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3-5z}{8}\\right) & \\le 8\\cdot (6-2z) \\\\ \\cancel{8}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3-5z}{\\cancel{8}}\\right) & \\le 8\\cdot (6-2z) \\\\ 3-5z & \\le 48-16z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-5z & \\le 48-16z \\\\ 3-5z+16z & \\le 48-16z+16z \\\\ 3+11z & \\le 48 \\\\ 3+11z-3 & \\le 48-3 \\\\ 11z & \\le 45 \\\\ \\frac{11z}{11} & \\le \\frac{45}{11} \\\\ z & \\le \\frac{45}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z\\le \\frac{45}{11}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072c003f-940f-5827-9c69-c6534a1061e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564952} {:answer/id #uuid "25a59e37-3156-5f96-a6d3-a085278a86ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564997} {:answer/id #uuid "638d7ca2-b9ee-56e3-ad94-7e22ab34a1e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565092} {:answer/id #uuid "ac923911-04d7-59bb-9992-7a8446c5e934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565194} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a09306-4232-5e19-a749-d592df6f358b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "638d7ca2-b9ee-56e3-ad94-7e22ab34a1e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565092} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d53ce85-93a3-5040-89c6-3357d95d5db8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d1348d32-c72c-5639-a597-5810a3541a7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342800" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2y={u}_{\\pi /3}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=-1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /3}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{s\\,{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{13}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$ \n\nHint: You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-\\pi s/3}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+9),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns=A({s}^{2}+9)+(Bs+C)(s+2).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n-2=A((-2)^{2}+9)+0\\,⟹\\,13A=-2\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{2}{13}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=\\frac{2}{13}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n0=9A+2C\\,⟹\\,0=9\\,\\left(-\\frac{2}{13}\\right)+2C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{9}{13}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{13}(-\\frac{2}{s+2}+\\frac{2s+9}{{s}^{2}+9})=\\frac{1}{13}(-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+2}+2\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}+3\\cdot \\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{13}(-2\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+2}\\}+2\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}\\}+3\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}\\}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(-2{e}^{-2t}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/3}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}[-2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}))+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(t-\\pi /3))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-\\pi /3))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}(-2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})(2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{13}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565471 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3519c2e5-5f0c-5be4-91e1-e42db2f66c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z>-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565017} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb378fe-4f71-5580-ab7e-5f601b873bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z>-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565047} {:answer/id #uuid "9926e2fb-fd74-51d3-bba5-349637b1d762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z<5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565170} {:answer/id #uuid "e606c3e3-7dab-5779-990d-e0e59050c9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z<-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565279} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0b25a1-2583-58a7-a151-48ea5344260a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z<7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3519c2e5-5f0c-5be4-91e1-e42db2f66c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z>-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565017} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb1ad4f1-13a1-54ac-b3a4-af4682c466cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dc1245c0-95c3-5566-8f75-3345f8116530" :question/math-academy-id "q-156054" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3z+1}{2}>\\frac{-28+6z}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3z+1}{2} & >\\frac{-28+6z}{7} \\\\ 14\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3z+1}{2}\\right) & >14\\cdot \\left(\\frac{-28+6z}{7}\\right) \\\\ 7\\cdot (3z+1) & >2\\cdot (-28+6z) \\\\ 21z+7 & >-56+12z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21z+7 & >-56+12z \\\\ 21z+7-12z & >-56+12z-12z \\\\ 9z+7 & >-56 \\\\ 9z+7-7 & >-56-7 \\\\ 9z & >-63 \\\\ \\frac{9z}{9} & >\\frac{-63}{9} \\\\ z & >-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z>-7.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d919e2a-d68a-568e-b91e-bde70fca882b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d919e2a-d68a-568e-b91e-bde70fca882b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565130} :answer-field/id #uuid "db32905c-5c7a-5bd1-bd14-25355dd7bb61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3e8207e-9140-5f5f-bfa7-ab83fc0d1420" :question/math-academy-id "q-334233" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{7}{9{x}^{7}}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{7}{9{x}^{7}}\\right) & =\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nThe positive factor of $\\frac{7}{9}$ does not change the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}=-\\infty .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "107579c4-226c-56f4-a2e2-579b1beca778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564965} {:answer/id #uuid "722274c3-742b-5a48-b51d-ae59fbe68344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565111} {:answer/id #uuid "991ed807-57db-5621-8684-6e0503cebcf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565169} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e1b4b5-e50a-5c14-9473-88dec5bbe338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565271} {:answer/id #uuid "fb160850-a523-56ec-9e9b-f5b031875117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb160850-a523-56ec-9e9b-f5b031875117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565322} :answer-field/id #uuid "358ca9bd-a430-5e36-accd-2c83f3de20f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "edf4a097-cde0-5ac1-86d1-cd429ba599d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95018" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} & 3{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{9}{2} & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} & =8 \\\\ 6{x}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8674d049-595e-5d07-a71e-5822b43b9d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8674d049-595e-5d07-a71e-5822b43b9d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565148} :answer-field/id #uuid "48a7ef4a-dd0b-5d40-ac05-a0608f5b503d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "efae9349-6eb8-5183-b15e-8510c6f912ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-250939" :question/problem "Find the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose sixth term is $-15$ and whose ninth term is $-\\frac{3}{25}.$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{9}=-\\frac{3}{25}$ and ${a}_{6}=-15.$ Substituting $n=9$ and $m=6$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{9-6} & =\\frac{{a}_{9}}{{a}_{6}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{3}{25}\\right)}{(-15)} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by solving the equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{125} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0406e5ab-8732-5f42-9dc0-2ef98837edfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{V}{9\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564946} {:answer/id #uuid "515340d0-d9cf-5eb4-ac80-59dfc5346185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{V}{3\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565055} {:answer/id #uuid "81410aa3-599c-5b7e-a1db-6dce30fb6236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=\\sqrt[3]{9\\pi V}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565138} {:answer/id #uuid "9d33a1a2-0563-576d-b3d4-649b89c427fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{9V}{\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565180} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d09076-162e-5ae2-8e86-9ff98030f326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3V}{\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d33a1a2-0563-576d-b3d4-649b89c427fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{9V}{\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565180} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb9d811f-3a95-59ec-9157-3f2c0587bc0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f5dc2452-94d3-5570-b907-2fef94df0654" :question/math-academy-id "q-82238" :question/problem "Find an expression for the height $h$ of a cylinder in terms of its volume $V$ given that the ratio of the radius of the base to the height of the cylinder is $\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that the ratio of the radius $r$ of the base to the height $h$ is $\\frac{1}{3},$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to express $h$ in terms of $V.$ Therefore, we need to isolate $r$ in the above equation, which we do using cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3r & =h \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting $r=\\frac{h}{3}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\ V & =\\pi {\\left(\\frac{h}{3}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot h \\\\ V & =\\pi \\cdot \\frac{{h}^{2}}{9}\\cdot h \\\\ V & =\\frac{\\pi {h}^{3}}{9} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =\\frac{9V}{\\pi } \\\\ h & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{9V}{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f585e57-5c50-5feb-9150-e466d09dad88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f585e57-5c50-5feb-9150-e466d09dad88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565049} :answer-field/id #uuid "71c427a6-d5aa-56d6-bd23-df19a31d1d11" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fc61e8f1-0b37-52d2-8620-d0e767062e04" :question/math-academy-id "q-334180" :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7f1d5de28c6479a2e9b8b33a6da25a4091b71b58f6dc0c535ef390c4cb97799.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $g(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=$\n {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(g(x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-2.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=-2\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1758365f-a72d-56cb-8910-850f490ea058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564975} {:answer/id #uuid "55a68899-fd6b-5e51-8741-e8646384dc54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565064} {:answer/id #uuid "6feaeecb-9e4c-51ac-9b20-5f7954edd8b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565106} {:answer/id #uuid "a65e4a14-81ca-54eb-9501-b356ec107bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565191} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f23a02-6b5e-5b17-bde8-5879ecbee74d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6feaeecb-9e4c-51ac-9b20-5f7954edd8b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565106} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3143b2c-7af2-5445-aaba-827a5cd2b88e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdb51987-aeea-5d6d-9fcd-a012fef3d69f" :question/math-academy-id "q-78081" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $-7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}}\\right) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-7\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =-7\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-7\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}=-7\\cdot 0=0\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ec9087c-76ac-5207-b1f9-ec6fe4b81431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{9}\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565729} {:answer/id #uuid "36010bc1-90bd-5d59-bf60-398650da4041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{9}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565762} {:answer/id #uuid "6e47a032-29fd-5008-bafe-e0cc57a28e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565852} {:answer/id #uuid "b130822a-20ed-52e4-9477-4de7e3cf757a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{9}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565957} {:answer/id #uuid "b7594013-bebf-56a0-a402-a6f6ee3a5b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{9}\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7594013-bebf-56a0-a402-a6f6ee3a5b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{9}\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565979} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d943c3b-30bf-5f50-9d21-212a996b1fd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09a2f783-f81f-5ba2-8e8b-78ad6330c2dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-61687" :question/problem "A radioactive substance decays exponentially such that its mass reduces by $\\frac{1}{4}$ every hour. If the mass of the substance was initially $2\\,\\text{kg},$ what is the mass of the substance after $9$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "If the mass reduces by $\\frac{1}{4},$ then $\\frac{3}{4}$ of its mass remains every hour.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ hour, the mass is $2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right).$\n\n- After $2$ hours, the mass is $2\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\n- After $3$ hours, the mass is $2\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{3}.$\n\n- $…$\n\nRepeating this pattern, we see that the mass after $9$ hours is $2\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{9}\\,\\text{kg}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e2a5228-4d34-5c57-8a6c-a3feb27ed440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565702} {:answer/id #uuid "2e001848-6114-55d4-898d-e807366debec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60-{(y-18)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565748} {:answer/id #uuid "4803ab05-0b65-5cb4-92e9-f03eef1b730f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565792} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4aefa9-79b2-5f3f-9f24-fa82ea18cebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{165}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565800} {:answer/id #uuid "564dbae7-bea0-5bb4-b219-b6f52f82ad54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}+{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e2a5228-4d34-5c57-8a6c-a3feb27ed440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565702} :answer-field/id #uuid "416ac210-0c5c-5b57-b96d-e01801ddf600" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52455c8c-f427-542e-9cf2-e8b407fdf74f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140407" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}+9y-21$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-9y+21)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-9y+21\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+21\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $21$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}+21\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}+21\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+9y-21 & =-({(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56025d08-3daa-57d3-a124-01b79cf0d75e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565815} {:answer/id #uuid "bed9968c-838f-5361-8f39-c03feaab5e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{120}^{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565984} {:answer/id #uuid "d0507e43-149d-5743-8d36-f39796b33425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566019} {:answer/id #uuid "db5a9956-eda8-532e-a69a-92488632bfd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{120}^{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566032} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2ae62b-708a-58b6-93f0-dc688cfbb87b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed2ae62b-708a-58b6-93f0-dc688cfbb87b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566067} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc24bef0-f8cf-5321-9a6d-d5127a9ed564" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c6f292d-8813-58fc-b4b8-7f51b18fc102" :question/math-academy-id "q-61703" :question/problem "A soccer club started with $120$ members. The number of members in the club then decreased by $\\frac{1}{5}$ every year. Which expression gives approximately the number of members after $3$ years?" :question/worked-solution "If the number of members reduced by $\\frac{1}{5}$ every year, it means that $\\frac{4}{5}$ of members remained every year.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ year, there were $120\\cdot \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)$ members.\n\n- After $2$ years, there were approximately $120\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2}$ members.\n\n- After $3$ years, there were approximately $120\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{3}$ members."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0c81066-fa93-57ad-9c31-169594b16f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0c81066-fa93-57ad-9c31-169594b16f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566076} :answer-field/id #uuid "321f92c3-450e-5fba-993b-2e46cd9808aa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566126} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ce5e56-780d-5c88-bed9-f6bd700d42ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01ce5e56-780d-5c88-bed9-f6bd700d42ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565679} :answer-field/id #uuid "78c39176-488f-543b-a2da-3abb77eaa53b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566154} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15719c4e-b7fb-5170-8bb8-2901fc8bc636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15719c4e-b7fb-5170-8bb8-2901fc8bc636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565714} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f40a4d7-d407-5619-8448-b04becc5c8a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5eff477c-d77b-5edc-9e57-fb4474642e96" :question/math-academy-id "q-218758" :question/problem "Rewrite $10{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{7}{10}$ by completing the square.\n\n$10{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{7}{10}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $10$. So, we take out a factor of $10$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n10({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{7}{100}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{2}}-\\,{\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{50}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}) & =10({(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{50}) \\\\  & =10{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+10\\cdot \\frac{3}{50} \\\\  & =10{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "383a1a6d-35ea-5270-9184-75c2682d7396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565769} {:answer/id #uuid "44526162-1855-508d-ad71-02db10f1e1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565788} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6d2b6a-5266-5c53-ac87-e02a1827047e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{10}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565802} {:answer/id #uuid "c97303ff-3ba1-59f2-bae9-fcada6af4564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566004} {:answer/id #uuid "f947d06a-0da6-5642-842f-83c69a159e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44526162-1855-508d-ad71-02db10f1e1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565788} :answer-field/id #uuid "57fbd290-f3b1-5227-bd0d-a92509c57f4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "71daf213-45ae-576f-97ae-9ffbff2f758a" :question/math-academy-id "q-52543" :question/problem "Rewrite $-2{x}^{2}-6x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2$. So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({x}^{2}+3x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}+3x) & =-2({(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{9}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70b54ca2-ba87-5256-9420-5d2e2bf07f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70b54ca2-ba87-5256-9420-5d2e2bf07f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565854} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7afddcb-dc24-5839-bcf0-80fc718004df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "83c46e33-f8bd-5862-a614-a54a16643b82" :question/math-academy-id "q-220358" :question/problem "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=(2x+k)(x-3k)$ is $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we will set our result equal to $\\frac{1}{4}.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=(2x+k)(x-3k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{k}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=3k.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{k}{2}+3k)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{6k-k}{2}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5k}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5k}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 5k & =1 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b2204f3-0b9c-5716-8b12-1daaa8e9c98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565695} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcec967-e7ae-5ddf-bf71-07068fba0434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(7x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{49}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565797} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9c5aa0-fb14-5496-9591-622a3ce17833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{63}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565829} {:answer/id #uuid "abd335e9-aa90-5300-ad70-e9bfcbdab375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(7x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565950} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a8f032-2fca-5906-b4f7-ab9bf75bf249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b9c5aa0-fb14-5496-9591-622a3ce17833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{63}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565829} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7c32e90-669a-52ff-9868-695c63e890c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3f44aac-b56c-541f-849d-0822403501d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-52539" :question/problem "Rewrite $7{x}^{2}-21x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $7$. So, we take out a factor of $7$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n7({x}^{2}-3x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7({x}^{2}-3x) & =7({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-7\\cdot \\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{63}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566273 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d9be715-36a9-5f87-aaff-b73f3c392482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565726} {:answer/id #uuid "64fb7564-0e54-5edc-bf06-22fc601176e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565835} {:answer/id #uuid "7625faaf-7a06-5f8d-9f7a-5f31afa9fee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565862} {:answer/id #uuid "82e0bdf4-a937-5979-9022-49f3096a8334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565884} {:answer/id #uuid "d5496e58-5442-54c8-8ae7-8fd4a6dda1d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82e0bdf4-a937-5979-9022-49f3096a8334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565884} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb17ec4b-63ee-5206-af27-3498015a3fd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f7c6f3ba-6533-5a1f-b464-492440ca8f6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163179" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}$\nand has mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nGiven that the center of mass is at $(1,2),$ find the moment of inertia about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =x{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass $(1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}((x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(x-1)^{2}y+{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}+4y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}-\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}+2{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{9}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}(x-1)^{3}+\\frac{9}{4}x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}-(-\\frac{3}{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008bf75e-050b-58ab-8f2d-0f365eb7a7d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566360} {:answer/id #uuid "039cf306-0126-5764-bf19-e0c056dcde07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566368} {:answer/id #uuid "78e6b7ad-d19e-52f9-b190-3f25cb33dd21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566593} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b6f41e-5756-5a52-8836-75961b8058f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566791} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e00566-bce8-541f-87f8-6188a4ebb0c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9e00566-bce8-541f-87f8-6188a4ebb0c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566863} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2f4d91a-c3cf-512e-99f9-72c16dd087ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c2e099a-ce97-51bf-a567-98e410e87149" :question/math-academy-id "q-156176" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\right)$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={337.5}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{15\\pi }{8}$ is equivalent to ${337.5}^{∘},$ which lies in the $4$th quadrant.\n\n![](images/a2/a248eaf8b200e2788db08164f77c2d3d457837e851726f54ca22f19cbc1dc440.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{15\\pi }{8}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =2\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{15\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\right)$ must be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\right)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0163c90f-63f7-520c-b90b-cdca85bc5861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566364} {:answer/id #uuid "78d2aa47-e366-536d-979b-7aaddb43183d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566591} {:answer/id #uuid "90f591ac-d503-53e4-9edf-df0ecead1c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566647} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8aa054-536b-5175-8e3d-21f8ecd92148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566710} {:answer/id #uuid "c62a4f9c-30eb-5f65-9868-b6c9578e4d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6y-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78d2aa47-e366-536d-979b-7aaddb43183d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566591} :answer-field/id #uuid "5af82be6-e454-517f-89c6-df6d183337b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1691ed28-0c5c-54a8-9012-9dfc00861f65" :question/math-academy-id "q-159719" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3y-2x}{7y-6x}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{(7y-6x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{3y-2x}{7y-6x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3y-2x)\\cdot (7y-6x)-(3y-2x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y-6x)}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (7y-6x)-(3y-2x)\\cdot (-6)}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14y+12x+18y-12x}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{(7y-6x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=4y.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e13dadc-ff81-528e-9c5a-0d82b2fa5105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{15}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566427} {:answer/id #uuid "2910a118-12d0-55a3-948e-ca1de0a828a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{15}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566452} {:answer/id #uuid "367a6d87-71d2-5ccd-b226-361c1c030e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{15}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566471} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6c1e2a-9d6a-584e-9fca-c8ec90fb2654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{15}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566600} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f2a19e-4ab6-5e5e-8696-8cf066687b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{15}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "367a6d87-71d2-5ccd-b226-361c1c030e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{15}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566471} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4cd172d-4fe9-5312-8123-d8cc7c56d90d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d922afd-bca6-504f-94e4-0636e349c3f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-156110" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{15}\\right)$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{15}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={132}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{11\\pi }{15}$ is equivalent to ${132}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/23/23a979aa6a346c2572ecc108e3b88ac4cbe3f192dc6818c975c9373cbca33e74.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{15}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{15}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{11\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{15}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{15}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{15}\\right)$ must be negative because tangent (and therefore cotangent) is always negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{15}\\right)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{15}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08050a00-d7c5-50f1-adb8-385171303ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566381} {:answer/id #uuid "13f66efb-6f99-576a-8818-22852dc76ccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566401} {:answer/id #uuid "48f0b34d-93c2-5f6e-a27c-884c035464a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566505} {:answer/id #uuid "d2420004-c2e0-5e7f-a0f9-afa99a76bcb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566776} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f43fbb-2eda-595f-9545-880fc4dbfa63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48f0b34d-93c2-5f6e-a27c-884c035464a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566505} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fefd2ee-4647-59e3-ba09-c04f194c181b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55481d4d-7e9b-585b-a201-3605d4c46b9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-105632" :question/problem "Calculate $∇\\cdot (F-G)$ for the vector fields $F$ and $G,$ where\n\n$$\nF=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\,i-\\frac{1}{y}\\,j,\\,∇\\cdot G=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (F-G) & =∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot (-G) \\\\  & =∇\\cdot F-∇\\cdot G.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(-\\frac{1}{y}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (F-G) & =∇\\cdot F-∇\\cdot G \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60243795-2a92-591c-983d-ab0a12df51fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566544} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccef17a-7e81-5ad2-b543-a2a7ee459876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-y)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566601} {:answer/id #uuid "b662e7db-0fcb-5572-a49f-f705511b1c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{csc}^{2}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566724} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f81ca3-bfba-5c9f-b61e-29a424ecf9f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566781} {:answer/id #uuid "e65931e8-e876-5d62-8009-c6a3aab5a425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60243795-2a92-591c-983d-ab0a12df51fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566544} :answer-field/id #uuid "a55eaa90-3c66-5bf4-a7e7-2ac1f5a2db6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d644ebc-b214-5b17-9daa-3ced5a3257b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-159712" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{{e}^{x+y}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient and chain rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y))\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y})}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot 1}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)){e}^{x+y}}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)={sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37dd99bc-3607-581f-978c-a71b67914c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}-2{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566477} {:answer/id #uuid "9126a86d-bcfd-5fec-9571-5503eba42ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566648} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d265ad-a1a6-58b0-b6d6-e96a59278366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}}-\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566688} {:answer/id #uuid "dafdca6c-9141-5612-9f79-f5c98e819afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566803} {:answer/id #uuid "f64dcd14-9eac-5db3-bba6-758aa5bc23bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}}{4}-\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{6}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9126a86d-bcfd-5fec-9571-5503eba42ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566648} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1056adf-eded-5bde-8128-44705a2baad4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "676d4494-4f46-573f-a890-2a8daa607201" :question/math-academy-id "q-113850" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of \n$\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}-x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}-x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{4/5}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int {x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/5+1}}{(\\frac{4}{5}+1)}-\\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{(1+1)}+2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{-4+1}}{(-4+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/5}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right)}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{-3}}{(-3)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1df86f71-4e8c-5223-a2c3-114b3c3a90ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566425} {:answer/id #uuid "23ed9481-6f88-5b6b-806c-7902c0dee17a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566439} {:answer/id #uuid "755654b4-0e0c-54a7-a879-4314f25dc630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566584} {:answer/id #uuid "79a314e6-0668-5c44-b3d1-02a3cf9ad5c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566595} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9ccd66-db58-59f7-af47-1c86f73f9c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23ed9481-6f88-5b6b-806c-7902c0dee17a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566439} :answer-field/id #uuid "789cacb9-1990-5456-9318-5cf3591eaa13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "738ade6c-0972-56b4-b1bb-9f8fdeb237e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-159661" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x+y}+{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y},$ evaluate ${f}_{y}(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x+y}+{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x+y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x+y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot (x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y))+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot (0+1)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(3,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3+1}}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7edfe9a2-e8ae-503f-bbdc-4826f3e0aeda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566604} {:answer/id #uuid "9f02033c-763b-5e0c-8fd5-990a639d71c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566671} {:answer/id #uuid "b6da8054-6941-55a6-9867-496063ddc2e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566725} {:answer/id #uuid "b92f1c93-ff3f-59e8-90e0-3827c745eefb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566731} {:answer/id #uuid "bb643e90-15c2-54f0-86b3-996299e8ff3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7edfe9a2-e8ae-503f-bbdc-4826f3e0aeda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566604} :answer-field/id #uuid "49f89144-238e-52a6-a3f7-a494782e66b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7db8f68b-e6bd-517c-b7bb-afe40f692949" :question/math-academy-id "q-156216" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={150}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ is equivalent to ${150}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\n![](images/d0/d04b5b75e4406104bd2621d54be389e87cb9e289199aac60db449fe3244a9b45.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ is in the $2$nd  quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)$ must be *negative* because the cosine ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e78b876-2391-5925-9f6b-e1686b55096a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e78b876-2391-5925-9f6b-e1686b55096a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566489} :answer-field/id #uuid "5961090d-3a51-57b9-a935-949cdac567c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8ea911ef-18d1-54db-a83b-3b4bb372d13e" :question/math-academy-id "q-353117" :question/problem "![](images/ee/eec15c94d63cab750a0dc15525d800452a1a1dbdd3e948dff8ef8d6cf0e2abc7.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the shaded region that lies between the polar curves $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]:$\n\n$$\n\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTo determine which is the inner function and which is the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/9b/9bcc7175d07a6d9bb4c3fa202f3ad711effa962e3c9a798b3869f9601d4214c3.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}((\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\theta )\\,{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(-\\sqrt{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67ebaae1-1b6f-54a0-9ee3-20ad6d94b297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-{x}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566561} {:answer/id #uuid "75869535-6c65-5fee-b563-b5239aaa4910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566587} {:answer/id #uuid "949d1af7-fd39-55bc-bf6f-81a0c846d362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566654} {:answer/id #uuid "a1102e39-908b-5d4b-9cc6-16a7726229bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566676} {:answer/id #uuid "e18db342-254e-56c4-b18d-3e730f819b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1102e39-908b-5d4b-9cc6-16a7726229bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566676} :answer-field/id #uuid "abc76652-94ff-55a2-98b8-14e731f2dc71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e4827b1-c8af-5e0e-a92c-bab0c6f19c7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71167" :question/problem "$\\int 5-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\left(\\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}\\right)-\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}+C \\\\  & =5x-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4500506d-0dc2-5226-b244-060346fe3405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566499} {:answer/id #uuid "8b93ef7a-8561-50f5-a232-6af32efb32cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566628} {:answer/id #uuid "93421e03-12b6-5005-87f8-a6ee58a03475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566651} {:answer/id #uuid "95354370-942a-523e-9f8d-b8a4123c078c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566657} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c91f1c-6eb6-5a8d-b48b-51444ed941ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95354370-942a-523e-9f8d-b8a4123c078c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566657} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f932fed-397b-5ace-b980-5e7fbe127e60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f664604-518d-5102-b04f-ac66274e4b60" :question/math-academy-id "q-156064" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={225}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ is equivalent to ${225}^{∘},$ which lies in the $3\\text{rd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/d6/d676fb0c9d75ee8230340203ec1a853e34384061ce899ddfc0185e49d2f8dbec.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\theta -\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)$ must be *positive* because the tangent ratio is *always* positive in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567073 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06327c3c-dce0-563c-b02b-f7d69ae0503a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}{5}+\\frac{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566371} {:answer/id #uuid "56759450-adc6-5b0d-a635-c7f96a6a0550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566525} {:answer/id #uuid "86252f96-8aa6-54b2-b5a8-c3649023555d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3}+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566616} {:answer/id #uuid "98759795-857b-56c3-8fe8-c710268b79fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}{2}+3\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566661} {:answer/id #uuid "ec410b0b-b1e5-506b-ad48-150967b9799d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}{5}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06327c3c-dce0-563c-b02b-f7d69ae0503a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}{5}+\\frac{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566371} :answer-field/id #uuid "dea66fd9-ec24-52e5-9e8a-c08de6b5d949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa2d5333-814d-52c3-9ed1-e83660135b41" :question/math-academy-id "q-49063" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\sqrt{x}(2+x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}(2+x)\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}(2+x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2{x}^{1/2}+{x}^{3/2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int {x}^{1/2}\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{3/2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}\\right)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}{5}+\\frac{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3179ff10-c8be-5032-b116-17b3d443db6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{7}{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566460} {:answer/id #uuid "51d8327b-b62f-54ce-a7a1-472117a5b25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566520} {:answer/id #uuid "52a5c30a-6d06-54eb-a7f8-2ebe257c1193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{7}{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566522} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef5f0ad-1feb-54d4-9d4a-009b294fb168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{7}{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566572} {:answer/id #uuid "cdcbdf16-64b9-50b1-9679-0fa5d22435fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3179ff10-c8be-5032-b116-17b3d443db6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{7}{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566460} :answer-field/id #uuid "69ab0297-0b7d-5f64-9cc7-f805d6068f4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3b5e67d-32a5-5a89-860d-1eeac5fbca6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-49059" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{5}+7}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{5}+7}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 7{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+7\\int {x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right)+7\\left(\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{7}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040b22cc-cfeb-583b-81a5-53f0dea47107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566369} {:answer/id #uuid "78edad60-37a0-5696-8808-78c28387a1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566594} {:answer/id #uuid "8d07ca4b-f8eb-5021-8b28-28339236a06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566633} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc566ec-859a-5f21-abf9-9853edc71d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566741} {:answer/id #uuid "d77afca8-c208-5bf5-94d2-109895490fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d77afca8-c208-5bf5-94d2-109895490fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566794} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1f4340c-600d-5355-bdc0-562f920def5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9712142-63a5-5fa8-9ab4-6ab895057764" :question/math-academy-id "q-156218" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={120}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ is equivalent to ${120}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2$nd  quadrant.\n\n![](images/34/342602449228f93042121b22f39d6cc203a6d945934a9ddb726e862838832815.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ is in the $2$nd  quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)$ must be *negative* because the cosine ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5137f900-8819-5d2b-870d-9336f074e8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566516} {:answer/id #uuid "8049c500-a93f-583f-9bcd-8b17d834f7e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566607} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f420e8-7277-5e0a-aae2-bb9d6c1656a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566683} {:answer/id #uuid "d28d88ff-ec44-58d2-9a11-04081769a148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566777} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c2a08c-128b-51b8-a4de-683e8f8d49cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3f420e8-7277-5e0a-aae2-bb9d6c1656a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566683} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1bb264d-a67d-5a32-839a-5ed29fa27f58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db4f44d3-9afa-5f71-9446-7889414ad8ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-156232" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{10}\\right)$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{10}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={198}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{11\\pi }{10}$ is equivalent to ${198}^{∘},$ which lies in the $3$rd quadrant.\n\n![](images/4f/4f66f8119626f97d6439aee81f5a02b1893a4dc2a787addc6c4a71ff865e3687.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{10}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{10}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\theta -\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{10}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{10}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{10}\\right)$ must be *negative* because the cosine ratio is *always* negative in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{10}\\right)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76bdef9c-f236-54e8-8836-4212e9f21172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566589} {:answer/id #uuid "894f79ee-fbb9-59fd-a530-5bbcfc38d4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566624} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9909aa-f561-5e4b-8e6f-a2dd3fe32fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566703} {:answer/id #uuid "b1037d50-5d56-585f-b784-5cc78be970d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566715} {:answer/id #uuid "f78d3c3a-e9ac-5f4f-9f6d-cd0c62e1f5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1037d50-5d56-585f-b784-5cc78be970d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566715} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd002646-d5bf-5b7f-a764-6a47744e5a08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7c752ad-04c5-54cf-9d34-168623b90a68" :question/math-academy-id "q-156044" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right)$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={112.5}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ is equivalent to ${112.5}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/9b/9b4b14f758b27c3bf9af0edec9fd6125fa268e4ec90b9138a986bb81afd48fc5.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{5\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right)$ must be *negative* because the tangent ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right)=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0362914a-2ba9-5072-860b-691c4653e0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566367} {:answer/id #uuid "24e234fa-fe7c-5c3d-aa0b-3bb70d1b07b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566441} {:answer/id #uuid "af519233-270c-5ed0-a399-5951152787df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566712} {:answer/id #uuid "ccbed2a2-cfc0-5c31-b2b0-68ccffcdd435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566766} {:answer/id #uuid "e4102b19-e608-50b0-be66-9cc06acf5fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af519233-270c-5ed0-a399-5951152787df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566712} :answer-field/id #uuid "717667f3-97e6-59a4-923d-5196f9eb79aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb9d502b-5792-5f23-8fe5-325b77e48b09" :question/math-academy-id "q-156095" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{5}\\right)$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{5}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={144}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{4\\pi }{5}$ is equivalent to ${144}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/d9/d9a7c21097737d5b9bcfcf81f7127651bb43bae15c77b617ef6e2ef039a339e5.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{5}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{4\\pi }{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{5}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{5}\\right)$ must be positive because sine (and therefore cosecant) is always positive in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{5}\\right)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2792cd75-5a84-53d7-8491-5b91dacadb1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566448} {:answer/id #uuid "42d06c19-f46b-5173-8df1-4ae98d82170b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566495} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7ef754-bbbc-5d5a-95b7-520a5ed902a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566771} {:answer/id #uuid "d748f2bb-f458-5ea8-90c9-04e882560096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566793} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e5a366-5d3c-5341-bf17-782bcbad1d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce7ef754-bbbc-5d5a-95b7-520a5ed902a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566771} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab1c585e-3588-50e2-ad40-f55869456e9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc822143-a882-56e7-a63c-7b3ea241404a" :question/math-academy-id "q-156141" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={135}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ is equivalent to ${135}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\n![](images/bb/bb29311d0e31708c7a3d9c8eb356b12531cb2e99af10a02bdf653c167346b438.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)$  must be *positive* because the sine ratio is *always* positive in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "851f7b6c-6ef8-5fa1-93c1-2b75578d0591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(1\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567328} {:answer/id #uuid "85a7bf6a-008a-5ad4-bb57-98a16c1ea300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(4+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567329} {:answer/id #uuid "98f4ca2a-9f76-510d-99d8-13b8550e9b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567331} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a6ea42-172d-5293-9420-f6734616457c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(2-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567336} {:answer/id #uuid "fa85603b-2370-5ab3-b834-146df244880e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(4\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98f4ca2a-9f76-510d-99d8-13b8550e9b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567331} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4ad85d6-9fc6-5588-8f2e-c1109fd32c75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28c24fc2-984b-5c1a-852c-201e6e077ee6" :question/problem "Consider the vector field \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =2\\left({x}^{2}yz\\right)\\,i \\\\ & \\quad +(2\\left({x}^{3}y\\right))\\,j \\\\ & \\quad +(2\\left(z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\right))\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& ∇\\cdot F \\\\ & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}  \\\\ & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\left({x}^{2}yz\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\left({x}^{3}y\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2\\left(z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\right))  \\\\ & =2\\left(2xyz+{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\text{div}\\,F(-1,-1,2) \\\\ & =2\\left(2(-1)(-1)(2)\\right. \\\\ & \\quad \\left. +(-1)^{3}\\right. \\\\ & \\quad \\left. +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-1}{-1}\\right)\\right)  \\\\ & =2\\left(4-1+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)  \\\\ & =2\\left(3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067d7587-bd7a-55ca-b2a9-817044886453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567349} {:answer/id #uuid "33f9719e-3e95-52b4-919a-e855b575c1c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567355} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9eb42f-0bfc-567b-a6cf-e1098750c4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567363} {:answer/id #uuid "9147b4dd-04fe-5fd1-8299-b6061cdd5d5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567373} {:answer/id #uuid "b5966bc5-1c82-5adb-b53d-5dcb7e743f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33f9719e-3e95-52b4-919a-e855b575c1c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567355} :answer-field/id #uuid "632e0419-a120-5f91-b9be-603360314bbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "337254b1-2fa0-54a0-9d70-76189ce2de0c" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(x^{2} y)\\,i+(x + 1)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(2 t)\\,i+(t + 1)\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2 t,\\quad y=t + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x^{2} y)\\,i+(x + 1)\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(2 t\\right)^{2} \\left(t + 1\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\left(2 t\\right) + 1\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =(4 t^{2} \\left(t + 1\\right))\\,i+(2 t + 1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t + 1)\\,j \\\\  & =(2)\\,i+(1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\left((4 t^{2} \\left(t + 1\\right))\\,i+(2 t + 1)\\,j\\right)\\cdot\\left((2)\\,i+(1)\\,j\\right) \\\\ &=(4 t^{2} \\left(t + 1\\right))\\cdot(2)+(2 t + 1)\\cdot(1) \\\\ &=(8 t^{3} + 8 t^{2})+(2 t + 1) \\\\ &=8 t^{3} + 8 t^{2} + 2 t + 1\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}(8 t^{3} + 8 t^{2} + 2 t + 1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[2 t^{4} + \\frac{8 t^{3}}{3} + t^{2} + t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=((1)+(1)+(2)+\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right))-((0)+(0)+(0)+(0)) \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{20}{3}\\right)-(0) \\\\ &=\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567368 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dc4ce1e-eafd-5783-9383-568f1601d09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left(-4-\\sqrt{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567358} {:answer/id #uuid "4bec57b6-4b6a-5921-a981-9e3f449068b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left(-2+\\sqrt{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567361} {:answer/id #uuid "8a03e0c2-20dc-5c9e-aa85-b421c94b43e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567371} {:answer/id #uuid "de8a8253-5270-5081-8567-22d741f3d493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left(1-\\sqrt{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567381} {:answer/id #uuid "f955c89d-840b-5d78-8735-84aef633daff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left(2-\\sqrt{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f955c89d-840b-5d78-8735-84aef633daff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left(2-\\sqrt{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567383} :answer-field/id #uuid "4702667f-3b1e-5bd1-81e7-1a8c1903dba7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "825d8f89-9569-5641-b2e2-7d7b47441607" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(-1)\\left((y\\operatorname{\\sin}x)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j\\right)$ and $C$ is the path  $r(t)=\\operatorname{arcsin}t\\,i+2j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}t,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t)) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=(-1)\\left(2\\operatorname{\\sin}(\\operatorname{arcsin}t)\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}t+2)\\,j\\right) \\\\ &=(-1)\\left(2t\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}t+2)\\,j\\right).\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{\\prime}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arcsin}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=(-1)\\left((2t\\,i+(\\operatorname{arcsin}t+2)\\,j)\\right)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,i) \\\\ &=(-1)\\left(\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-1)\\left({\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\,dt\\right) \\\\  & =(-1)\\left(-2\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2}\\right) \\\\  & =(-1)\\left(-2(\\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}}-1)\\right) \\\\  & =(-1)\\left(2-\\sqrt{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48f3edd2-28d1-5513-87e8-c881765ba4a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}(i-3\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567432} {:answer/id #uuid "99c9a946-38a2-55a3-8063-dbffd3f7fb0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567440} {:answer/id #uuid "b1dfeede-c920-57a7-84b3-7ef22805a200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567445} {:answer/id #uuid "e74375bc-1ad7-51fa-973a-ad68c92af852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}(3\\,i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567452} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ec0fd9-64f5-5f3c-a3bb-aad8ae20b645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}(i+3\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0ec0fd9-64f5-5f3c-a3bb-aad8ae20b645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}(i+3\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567454} :answer-field/id #uuid "214b114b-d57c-50b7-9ca4-13d6e7035711" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "161f4c79-57b1-5774-afbb-2d7aca169df7" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point  where $(x,y)=(3,1)$, can be found by computing $∇g(3,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(3,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & ={g}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{g}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y\\,i+\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot x\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\,j \\\\ ∇g(3,1) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{(3)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{(1)}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇g(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇g(3,1)}{‖∇g(3,1)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{2}+(1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}(i+3\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285af7d1-a87a-5625-a6b6-d8dcc568f173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\,i+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567425} {:answer/id #uuid "527d27a2-bb71-572c-8834-d84b72906ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}(2\\,i-3\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567434} {:answer/id #uuid "626368b7-98b7-50d4-9eb8-f70103c98cf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567436} {:answer/id #uuid "91db4913-a017-59b5-8d28-26fcc2fa2d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}(2\\,i+3\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567439} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3ec1b9-2e36-56c3-a2f7-cbd42322dc44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}(3\\,i+2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91db4913-a017-59b5-8d28-26fcc2fa2d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}(2\\,i+3\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567439} :answer-field/id #uuid "34900a04-64dc-5527-90bc-fb1ee330dcb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33daf655-8020-5d37-af88-f1b07a868f33" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(9,16).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(9,16)$ can be found by computing $∇f(9,16).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(9,16):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\right)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(9,16) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{9}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16}}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\,i+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(2\\,i+3\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(9,16):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(9,16)}{‖∇f(9,16)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{12}(2\\,i+3\\,j)}{‖\\frac{1}{12}(2\\,i+3\\,j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(3)^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}(2\\,i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}(2\\,i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}(2\\,i+3\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fa7421b-25b5-54d9-abc5-b3f30bf4e924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{194}}{194}(-5\\,i+13\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567426} {:answer/id #uuid "377104d7-3a96-5797-8a46-52faa5fe3930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{194}}{194}(13\\,i-5\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567429} {:answer/id #uuid "4152bee6-a103-5066-9916-e8ec290e17bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{12}\\,i-\\frac{5}{12}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567431} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a8d96b-d251-5b81-816c-277b51ae94ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{18}\\,i-\\frac{5}{18}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567446} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b431a5-1b4e-55e9-be8b-e4d0913658c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{194}}{194}(13\\,i+5\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "377104d7-3a96-5797-8a46-52faa5fe3930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{194}}{194}(13\\,i-5\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567429} :answer-field/id #uuid "280e4d68-ea50-53bf-b04a-a692f502867f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3e908d9-bc89-55b4-a12b-976025ac1c38" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(13,5).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(13,5)$, can be found by computing $∇f(13,5).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(13,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(13,5) & =\\left(\\frac{(13)}{\\sqrt{(13)^{2}-(5)^{2}}}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\left(-\\frac{(5)}{\\sqrt{(13)^{2}-(5)^{2}}}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{\\sqrt{144}}\\,i-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{144}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{12}\\,i-\\frac{5}{12}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(13,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(13,5)}{‖∇f(13,5)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{13}{12}\\,i-\\frac{5}{12}\\,j}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{13}{12}\\right)^{2}+\\left(-\\frac{5}{12}\\right)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{13}{12}\\,i-\\frac{5}{12}\\,j}{\\frac{\\sqrt{194}}{12}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{194}}{194}(13\\,i-5\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "061cb6cc-3959-5b14-a981-3106b485c5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567421} {:answer/id #uuid "3e51dd0e-8bba-5b42-b988-422251d36d75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567430} {:answer/id #uuid "58e6f7ff-07ca-5404-a2f8-04a18016cc0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567435} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9ab139-6632-5ccc-9f6f-ba0573cb2c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567441} {:answer/id #uuid "a337bb17-7b8c-5901-9a49-487470e0c389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "061cb6cc-3959-5b14-a981-3106b485c5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567421} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcc9e5b3-fcfc-594a-bbf3-8c741fb6bf4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b488d0d8-0d5c-58db-a90e-48c42f1d42c6" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,0)$, can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =\\left(\\frac{(1)}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}-(0)^{2}}}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\left(-\\frac{(0)}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}-(0)^{2}}}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}\\,i-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{1}}\\,j \\\\  & =i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,0)}{‖∇f(1,0)‖} & =\\frac{i}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{i}{1} \\\\  & =i\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "516067a1-6bca-5069-bbab-140935f51b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567433} {:answer/id #uuid "86eaf185-0d45-569a-a7a0-8dff4d65f888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567437} {:answer/id #uuid "8e50f7c7-1827-5994-b8f2-c35e30b7dddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567438} {:answer/id #uuid "9d56dc45-fddc-52b7-8158-e2e3313891b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567442} {:answer/id #uuid "d514d19a-83e2-56dc-ae0c-76ff2bae7b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d56dc45-fddc-52b7-8158-e2e3313891b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567442} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4faeac1-9e8b-5c11-aec7-607387173f75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db955fef-1148-5a1c-a192-f4c8263ee71a" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(4,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(4,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right))\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\right)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(4,0) & =\\left(-\\frac{(0)}{(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\left(\\frac{(4)}{(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{16}\\,i+\\frac{4}{16}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(4,0)}{‖∇f(4,0)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}(j)}{‖\\frac{1}{4}(j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(1)^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}(j) \\\\  & =j\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186568226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b221b9-03f6-5ca6-beba-1508c338f7f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568215} {:answer/id #uuid "1031a37d-3139-5e7e-bd91-9947b2ecd733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568216} {:answer/id #uuid "30ead38b-20c2-5594-a0fd-7b24e0a712e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568221} {:answer/id #uuid "75ca8635-d921-55ac-bc68-e4b1e06608a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568231} {:answer/id #uuid "e0feead4-55c2-5b19-8ac0-7aba1e372e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06b221b9-03f6-5ca6-beba-1508c338f7f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568215} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d697206-93f6-5a50-a668-a0f2fda200b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d3550f0-34e9-5b93-82ae-f29695f07079" :question/problem "Given that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\tan^{2} x = \\frac{16}{81}$ for $0 \\le x < \\pi$, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, rounded to one decimal place?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Taking the square root of both sides of the equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan^2 x &=\\frac{16}{81} \\\\ \\tan x &=\\pm \\frac{4}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have to solve the following two equations:\n\n$$\n\\tan x=-\\frac{4}{9}, \\qquad \\tan x=\\frac{4}{9}\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\tan x=-\\frac{4}{9}$. The solution for $0\\le x<\\pi$ is $x=\\pi-\\arctan\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)$, or $x\\approx 2.723368$.\n- Then, we solve $\\tan x=\\frac{4}{9}$. The solution for $0\\le x<\\pi$ is $x=\\arctan\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)$, or $x\\approx 0.418224$.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\nx_1\\approx 0.418224,\\qquad x_2\\approx 2.723368\n$$\n\nFinally, using the unrounded values,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x_1\\cdot x_2 &=\\arctan\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)\\bigl(\\pi-\\arctan\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)\\bigr) \\\\ &\\approx 1.138979 \\\\ &\\approx 1.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186568227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cccb757-a52e-5e29-800e-cbef649558d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568220} {:answer/id #uuid "3564fddc-70a9-5c5f-b4f5-a1734844e007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568222} {:answer/id #uuid "74b880c7-45d0-5e9d-98c3-380b410ec885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568230} {:answer/id #uuid "766a6175-39ae-51be-a5d5-6eb89fc57a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568232} {:answer/id #uuid "f7929a52-e616-5133-9ebf-6e0a41d60a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3564fddc-70a9-5c5f-b4f5-a1734844e007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568222} :answer-field/id #uuid "b857ab3e-cc46-5081-89ca-49ab8c46e4b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f0967de-0e0e-58c5-8767-e2c070bf7b46" :question/problem "Given that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\tan^{2} x = \\frac{1}{4}$ for $0 \\le x < \\pi$, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, rounded to one decimal place?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Taking the square root of both sides of the equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan^2 x &=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\tan x &=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have to solve the following two equations:\n\n$$\n\\tan x=-\\frac{1}{2}, \\qquad \\tan x=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\tan x=-\\frac{1}{2}$. The solution for $0\\le x<\\pi$ is $x=\\pi-\\arctan\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$, or $x\\approx 2.677945$.\n- Then, we solve $\\tan x=\\frac{1}{2}$. The solution for $0\\le x<\\pi$ is $x=\\arctan\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$, or $x\\approx 0.463648$.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\nx_1\\approx 0.463648,\\qquad x_2\\approx 2.677945\n$$\n\nFinally, using the unrounded values,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x_1\\cdot x_2 &=\\arctan\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\bigl(\\pi-\\arctan\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\bigr) \\\\ &\\approx 1.241623 \\\\ &\\approx 1.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186568235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22fb2869-c80b-57fa-bf3f-07702724e466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568219} {:answer/id #uuid "3c12e8ef-90d4-5a67-8a1c-23c0f390179c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568224} {:answer/id #uuid "8753ea32-a232-5092-ab7b-4c131e40a820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568234} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb775ec-daab-554d-b749-e01f8d21f2b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568243} {:answer/id #uuid "edaa2ef5-274e-51ec-bea4-87f7cd459519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8753ea32-a232-5092-ab7b-4c131e40a820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568234} :answer-field/id #uuid "df90002f-c381-56eb-a835-f4b010898e44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c15c682-35d6-59cf-83a9-7b09d89e88ff" :question/problem "Given that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\tan^{2} x = \\frac{81}{4}$ for $0 \\le x < \\pi$, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, rounded to one decimal place?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Taking the square root of both sides of the equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan^2 x &=\\frac{81}{4} \\\\ \\tan x &=\\pm \\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have to solve the following two equations:\n\n$$\n\\tan x=-\\frac{9}{2}, \\qquad \\tan x=\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\tan x=-\\frac{9}{2}$. The solution for $0\\le x<\\pi$ is $x=\\pi-\\arctan\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)$, or $x\\approx 1.789465$.\n- Then, we solve $\\tan x=\\frac{9}{2}$. The solution for $0\\le x<\\pi$ is $x=\\arctan\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)$, or $x\\approx 1.352127$.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\nx_1\\approx 1.352127,\\qquad x_2\\approx 1.789465\n$$\n\nFinally, using the unrounded values,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x_1\\cdot x_2 &=\\arctan\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)\\bigl(\\pi-\\arctan\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)\\bigr) \\\\ &\\approx 2.419585 \\\\ &\\approx 2.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186568238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20cb51ce-d9d5-5ade-8df5-068dd693a317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568218} {:answer/id #uuid "48e177dd-ec6c-52ca-a96b-bb78f6f78599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568225} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ac1142-34e9-5a44-99d1-95b47f1d7196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568236} {:answer/id #uuid "ace1d1b1-821a-5e1e-82b7-42153aff7a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568239} {:answer/id #uuid "b672af62-e33e-512e-8b32-97287ca76dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ace1d1b1-821a-5e1e-82b7-42153aff7a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568239} :answer-field/id #uuid "383bcf23-2533-5b79-9825-c9f32a85de3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a90d8ac2-fbf6-52cb-9152-6e2839b823ca" :question/problem "Given that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\tan^{2} x = \\frac{64}{9}$ for $0 \\le x < \\pi$, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, rounded to one decimal place?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Taking the square root of both sides of the equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan^2 x &=\\frac{64}{9} \\\\ \\tan x &=\\pm \\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have to solve the following two equations:\n\n$$\n\\tan x=-\\frac{8}{3}, \\qquad \\tan x=\\frac{8}{3}\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\tan x=-\\frac{8}{3}$. The solution for $0\\le x<\\pi$ is $x=\\pi-\\arctan\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)$, or $x\\approx 1.929567$.\n- Then, we solve $\\tan x=\\frac{8}{3}$. The solution for $0\\le x<\\pi$ is $x=\\arctan\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)$, or $x\\approx 1.212026$.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\nx_1\\approx 1.212026,\\qquad x_2\\approx 1.929567\n$$\n\nFinally, using the unrounded values,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x_1\\cdot x_2 &=\\arctan\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)\\bigl(\\pi-\\arctan\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)\\bigr) \\\\ &\\approx 2.338685 \\\\ &\\approx 2.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186568245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c496924-6993-585b-920c-4f2bd086a8b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568217} {:answer/id #uuid "74554212-0cae-591c-b8f5-f2d84dab9d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568229} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce44376-3136-5698-bd44-283fbb93f51c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568233} {:answer/id #uuid "a861cdfc-f538-59ee-9d8d-a168901f84fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568237} {:answer/id #uuid "c003d69c-d6bd-5b9a-8764-31795bfcc2dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ce44376-3136-5698-bd44-283fbb93f51c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568233} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7a0541f-4b71-5e02-a12e-190a45bfc6d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186568241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3dec242-c170-5258-becc-1d630e0cfadf" :question/problem "Given that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\tan^{2} x = \\frac{64}{81}$ for $0 \\le x < \\pi$, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, rounded to one decimal place?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Taking the square root of both sides of the equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan^2 x &=\\frac{64}{81} \\\\ \\tan x &=\\pm \\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have to solve the following two equations:\n\n$$\n\\tan x=-\\frac{8}{9}, \\qquad \\tan x=\\frac{8}{9}\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\tan x=-\\frac{8}{9}$. The solution for $0\\le x<\\pi$ is $x=\\pi-\\arctan\\left(\\frac{8}{9}\\right)$, or $x\\approx 2.414950$.\n- Then, we solve $\\tan x=\\frac{8}{9}$. The solution for $0\\le x<\\pi$ is $x=\\arctan\\left(\\frac{8}{9}\\right)$, or $x\\approx 0.726642$.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\nx_1\\approx 0.726642,\\qquad x_2\\approx 2.414950\n$$\n\nFinally, using the unrounded values,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x_1\\cdot x_2 &=\\arctan\\left(\\frac{8}{9}\\right)\\bigl(\\pi-\\arctan\\left(\\frac{8}{9}\\right)\\bigr) \\\\ &\\approx 1.754805 \\\\ &\\approx 1.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "463e6283-fe84-591f-ab90-92bd3bf2ca2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{183}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569359} {:answer/id #uuid "6c313c29-a9fa-50a3-a72f-f9f97ad5fd19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{195}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569363} {:answer/id #uuid "735b965c-278e-506b-b743-6b4c5751059a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{187}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569364} {:answer/id #uuid "8cdb34af-12c7-5473-9552-03124c9fd7fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{191}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569368} {:answer/id #uuid "e2430b00-5c3d-545c-9a17-fff45ac0fc3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{199}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c313c29-a9fa-50a3-a72f-f9f97ad5fd19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{195}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569363} :answer-field/id #uuid "79552a87-0677-5913-828b-e13c00090e3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3510f020-ef92-5354-86ff-374ac770636a" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{7}x - 4y &=21 \\\\ \\frac{12}{7}x + 4y &=43\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $\\frac{10}{7}x - 7y$?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We will solve the system using the elimination method.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $1$, then we will get a $4y$ term that can cancel with the $-4y$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{7}x - 4y &=21 \\\\ \\frac{12}{7}x + 4y &=43 \\quad\\mid\\;\\cdot(1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{7}x - 4y &= 21 \\\\ \\frac{12}{7}x + 4y &= 43 \\\\ (\\frac{4}{7} + \\frac{12}{7})x &= 64 \\\\ \\frac{16}{7}x &= 64 \\\\ x &= 28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substitute $x = 28$ into one of the original equations to solve for $y$. We'll use the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{7}x + 4y &= 43 \\\\ \\frac{12}{7}(28) + 4y &= 43 \\\\ 48 + 4y &= 43 \\\\ 4y &= -5 \\\\ y &= -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $\\frac{10}{7}x - 7y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{7}x - 7y &= \\frac{10}{7}(28) - 7\\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right) \\\\ &= 40 + \\frac{35}{4} \\\\ &= \\frac{160 + 35}{4} \\\\ &= \\frac{195}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the value of $\\frac{10}{7}x - 7y$ is $\\frac{195}{4}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569358 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf76111-b39d-59c4-aac8-32ec7129f3c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569349} {:answer/id #uuid "37a1d43a-4327-50ee-a106-5d8659e42e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569356} {:answer/id #uuid "4d62bdf1-95f4-537d-b3a4-4e2c0d2af737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569360} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c4275a-c738-5b78-afdd-1e48ed14aa59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569370} {:answer/id #uuid "f2abac86-59bd-5280-b8ea-8655fdf535fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2c4275a-c738-5b78-afdd-1e48ed14aa59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569370} :answer-field/id #uuid "c814bf37-a436-58d5-ad34-3934aa979755" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d3dcfc2-1724-5d2d-8d76-921e18cec113" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}y + 4x &=-22 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}y - 4x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $\\frac{1}{6}y + 6x$?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We will solve the system using the elimination method.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $1$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}y + 4x &=-22 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}y - 4x &=0 \\quad\\mid\\;\\cdot(1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}y + 4x &= -22 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}y - 4x &= 0 \\\\ (\\frac{5}{3} + \\frac{1}{6})y &= -22 \\\\ \\frac{11}{6}y &= -22 \\\\ y &= -12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substitute $y = -12$ into one of the original equations to solve for $x$. We'll use the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}y - 4x &= 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}(-12) - 4x &= 0 \\\\ -2 - 4x &= 0 \\\\ -4x &= 2 \\\\ x &= -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $\\frac{1}{6}y + 6x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}y + 6x &= \\frac{1}{6}(-12) + 6\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ &= -2 - 3 \\\\ &= -5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the value of $\\frac{1}{6}y + 6x$ is $-5$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569361 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13ae9c99-2ad1-5f9c-a374-4dc5bd0c1ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569351} {:answer/id #uuid "63e19ad0-ede0-5eab-92f3-f8c8a64b5dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569362} {:answer/id #uuid "8b16075f-c179-5916-86c9-62d2d995ace4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569367} {:answer/id #uuid "c62b87d5-83ca-5c09-8805-9e06d409860f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569371} {:answer/id #uuid "e13b6f2c-2b40-531b-8f4d-19b70b293770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e13b6f2c-2b40-531b-8f4d-19b70b293770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569373} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e1f7276-d007-5d0d-999b-2556abb43647" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "529b52a2-e8ec-5d9c-ae2f-4e0f1d414c59" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}y + 2x &=11 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}y - 2x &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $\\frac{19}{5}y + 2x$?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We will solve the system using the elimination method.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $1$, then we will get a $-2x$ term that can cancel with the $2x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}y + 2x &=11 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}y - 2x &=-3 \\quad\\mid\\;\\cdot(1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}y + 2x &= 11 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}y - 2x &= -3 \\\\ (\\frac{3}{5} + \\frac{1}{5})y &= 8 \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}y &= 8 \\\\ y &= 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substitute $y = 10$ into one of the original equations to solve for $x$. We'll use the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}y - 2x &= -3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(10) - 2x &= -3 \\\\ 2 - 2x &= -3 \\\\ -2x &= -5 \\\\ x &= \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $\\frac{19}{5}y + 2x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{19}{5}y + 2x &= \\frac{19}{5}(10) + 2\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) \\\\ &= 38 + 5 \\\\ &= 43\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the value of $\\frac{19}{5}y + 2x$ is $43$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c823551-26e2-5047-95b1-7935b5424578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569347} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc31954-58e3-5b2d-895e-355ade44f008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569353} {:answer/id #uuid "34de7ba9-40ed-5b42-adc6-395740ead635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569354} {:answer/id #uuid "eacf88d2-a7cb-553f-acb9-494acd95de4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569375} {:answer/id #uuid "f99bdcdc-46ae-5005-bbfb-70cfdc1ea22d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f99bdcdc-46ae-5005-bbfb-70cfdc1ea22d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569377} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cd1fabf-4b9e-54b9-bbe2-7e26a9c0ab68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ff2d47e-9829-5fb0-94d9-ffdd4e63facb" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{7}x - 3y &=-46 \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}x + 3y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $\\frac{5}{7}x - 12y$?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We will solve the system using the elimination method.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $1$, then we will get a $3y$ term that can cancel with the $-3y$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{7}x - 3y &=-46 \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}x + 3y &=-2 \\quad\\mid\\;\\cdot(1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{7}x - 3y &= -46 \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}x + 3y &= -2 \\\\ (\\frac{12}{7} + \\frac{4}{7})x &= -48 \\\\ \\frac{16}{7}x &= -48 \\\\ x &= -21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substitute $x = -21$ into one of the original equations to solve for $y$. We'll use the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{7}x + 3y &= -2 \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}(-21) + 3y &= -2 \\\\ -12 + 3y &= -2 \\\\ 3y &= 10 \\\\ y &= \\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $\\frac{5}{7}x - 12y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{7}x - 12y &= \\frac{5}{7}(-21) - 12\\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right) \\\\ &= -15 - 40 \\\\ &= -55\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the value of $\\frac{5}{7}x - 12y$ is $-55$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4188061c-4dfe-5f94-b792-4dca2da6c67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569532} {:answer/id #uuid "45c85e36-fafb-5cd0-bf93-acbfc1d73fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569539} {:answer/id #uuid "68868532-f794-5090-9203-a2b2d0d74f1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569606} {:answer/id #uuid "b59463e4-063b-5b43-ba22-7b25a136607c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569732} {:answer/id #uuid "df928123-92fc-5c8f-ae4f-3bf6ff6d7789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df928123-92fc-5c8f-ae4f-3bf6ff6d7789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569798} :answer-field/id #uuid "040b322c-582e-5726-82e2-05dc9e18e0aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0298a94b-23a5-5b8c-89b1-b11dd1bda638" :question/math-academy-id "q-89150" :question/problem "What is the expected value of one roll of a four-sided die?" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the roll of a four-sided die. Then the support is $S=\\{1,2,3,4\\},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\frac{1}{4}:$\n\n$$\nf(1)=f(2)=f(3)=f(4)=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)++3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =1\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+4\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2+3+4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{4} \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bec36d6-dfb7-5749-9664-f7f73dc25b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\$10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569485} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9bbeaf-98e6-558c-81d6-d0813fc9f4ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\$19$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569509} {:answer/id #uuid "59a5f4bc-a0cc-5d8a-b1c9-87ca178ebcf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\$7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569572} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4ee2a5-d371-5586-98ed-838864441766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\$9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569669} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e0eb6a-bd70-5f35-a14d-f4f095208cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\$8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1e0eb6a-bd70-5f35-a14d-f4f095208cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\$8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569722} :answer-field/id #uuid "53865da6-8e44-5bb9-8d40-4d2805caf8a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "068e9bc7-6f46-5ac9-a057-d5f4aa9a8aeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-127818" :question/problem "One way to bet in roulette is to place $\\$1$ on a single number. If the ball lands on that number, the player wins $\\$35.$ If the ball lands on any of the other $37$ slots, the player doesn't win. What is the expected profit, rounded to the nearest dollar, for a player who plays one hundred games on number $12$ in a row?" :question/worked-solution "First of all, profit is revenue minus cost, or in our case, winnings minus cost. So, once we have found the expected winnings after one hundred games, we need to subtract $100\\cdot 1=100$ dollars to account for the price of playing.\n\nNow, let's find the expected winnings and summarize them in a table. If $W$ is the winnings (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(w)$ is the probability mass function for $W,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(w)$ | $\\frac{1}{38}$ | $\\frac{37}{38}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $w$ | $35$ | $0$ |\n\nThe expected value of $W$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =35\\cdot f(35)+0\\cdot f(0) \\\\  & =35\\left(\\frac{1}{38}\\right)+0\\left(\\frac{37}{38}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{38} \\\\  & ≈0.92.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the expected winnings for a single game. So, the expected winnings for $100$ games  on number $12$ is\n\n$$\n100\\cdot \\text{E}[W]=100\\cdot (0.92)=92.\n$$\n\nNow, subtracting the $100$ dollars paid to play, we get that the expected profit (in dollars) is\n\n$$\nE[p]=92-100=-8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70f01508-769b-5ace-9917-5f7722cd7ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569628} {:answer/id #uuid "846fb0c9-3ec1-54f5-b33c-0e023a6c3914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569662} {:answer/id #uuid "864177d3-700a-5680-a7ae-fe5e85113f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569665} {:answer/id #uuid "c63e937b-281c-584f-875d-739cf8a9c9da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569758} {:answer/id #uuid "f0214696-0b2a-5443-87e4-6a0873c5d70e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "846fb0c9-3ec1-54f5-b33c-0e023a6c3914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569662} :answer-field/id #uuid "fce87f54-6287-5426-bb0b-5253c4ba2656" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1355c292-a399-578d-8bac-2939b3befda0" :question/math-academy-id "q-145039" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\infty$\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right){cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{+},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\cancel{cos}\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/50/50637a502a56fc242ba5d8e2fda59ddc899295d4b14f7ae692869536b95ef661.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f39199e8-353c-5632-85b5-146e5d346bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f39199e8-353c-5632-85b5-146e5d346bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569826} :answer-field/id #uuid "4197adf1-104f-52af-a2fe-ce58ae6658aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "267e0e90-b604-5e86-ad43-795951237ad2" :question/math-academy-id "q-337346" :question/problem "One way to bet in roulette is to place $\\$2$ on a single number. If the ball lands on that number, the player wins $\\$70.$ If the ball lands on any of the other $37$ slots, the player doesn't win. What is the expected profit, rounded to the nearest dollar, for a player who plays one hundred games on number $8$ in a row?\n\nThe expected profit in dollars is $E(p)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First of all, profit is revenue minus cost, or in our case, winnings minus cost. So, once we have found the expected winnings after one hundred games, we need to subtract $100\\cdot 2=200$ dollars to account for the price of playing.\n\nNow, let's find the expected winnings and summarize them in a table. If $W$ is the winnings (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(w)$ is the probability mass function for $W,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(w)$ | $\\frac{1}{38}$ | $\\frac{37}{38}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $w$ | $70$ | $0$ |\n\nThe expected value of $W$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =70\\cdot f(70)+0\\cdot f(0) \\\\  & =70\\left(\\frac{1}{38}\\right)+0\\left(\\frac{37}{38}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{70}{38} \\\\  & ≈1.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the expected winnings for a single game. So, the expected winnings for $100$ games on number $8$ is\n\n$$\n100\\cdot \\text{E}[W]=100\\cdot (1.84)=184.\n$$\n\nNow, subtracting the $200$ dollars paid to play, we get that the expected profit (in dollars) is\n\n$$\nE[p]=184-200=-16.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e88955e-5bc1-5000-8512-3eeafc61d0f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569587} {:answer/id #uuid "6238dcb6-e730-5761-a8fc-314e6a8b5732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569597} {:answer/id #uuid "751d8865-5818-58ec-a5f2-b7595be506a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569638} {:answer/id #uuid "a54b68c4-6f83-5b6d-a8d5-c61dfd48387e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569709} {:answer/id #uuid "bc748a1d-aa55-53b1-8727-38e8aeaf186e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6238dcb6-e730-5761-a8fc-314e6a8b5732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569597} :answer-field/id #uuid "d78da839-c447-5f6a-b9f4-52fee3e7c783" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29efc143-8ef6-514b-b08b-b09cf3c6dccf" :question/math-academy-id "q-64861" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ee243d-a0f6-58ea-a3e3-0cc51073be09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{27.42}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569454} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa4e6b5-7b61-5d05-8391-af677ab50fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{26.38}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569624} {:answer/id #uuid "88480d0d-769a-530a-9275-5454a6d74513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{23.62}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569668} {:answer/id #uuid "9914af0e-f526-50f6-98f8-1152e4ec30ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{20.73}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569688} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd7adc3-cc26-59cb-97d3-e0576be8c6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{29.38}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fa4e6b5-7b61-5d05-8391-af677ab50fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{26.38}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569624} :answer-field/id #uuid "264c3506-720d-5be8-870e-eb9352af9f27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39fc3bcb-e781-5ee0-b172-9c69c7f5c440" :question/math-academy-id "q-201" :question/problem "A triangle has sides $a$, $b$, and $c.$ The sides $a$, $b$, and $c$ are of lengths  $9$, $12$, and $18$, respectively. What is the measure of the angle between the sides $b$ and $c?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](images/de/dede0326d992509dedf1c3a2854b47076ccc48ebf0e07705c8c12762d8ecdead.png)\n\nWe use the Law of Cosines to solve for angle $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡A & =\\frac{{b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-{a}^{2}}{2bc}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\na=9,\\,b=12,\\,c=18.\n$$\n\nThis gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡A & =\\frac{{12}^{2}+{18}^{2}-{9}^{2}}{2(12)(18)} \\\\  & =\\frac{144+324-81}{432} \\\\  & =\\frac{387}{432}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠F & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{387}{432}\\right) \\\\  & ={26.38}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe0a563-e910-5a09-9b65-423c48c76310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569468} {:answer/id #uuid "4ede0597-e201-5e81-953f-0df38ea579f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569556} {:answer/id #uuid "7a918a37-015b-582f-9559-4c91adb530ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569645} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b00882-095d-51f2-92e9-ed2560cdc8b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569701} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f0df0d-c797-5e92-9ea6-b642a07c5dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ede0597-e201-5e81-953f-0df38ea579f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569556} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca41ebba-af00-5d55-bf09-af4bba36416b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f8bf9ef-a3d1-5fa9-97cc-d10fddcf1cd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-144911" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta (5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right){cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\cancel{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{{cos}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-3)}{0\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/d7/d7881ba860bcc32301a162eb200ea9075b06b63c780be74c14aedbcb1654eaaa.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3a5e633-756d-5e3a-8fa9-0ed6225a69ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3a5e633-756d-5e3a-8fa9-0ed6225a69ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569705} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e2ebfb-2990-504c-a3b7-16d650b15125" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41c4d9f2-fb24-5742-893b-e72bc5478b30" :question/math-academy-id "q-276300" :question/problem "A quality control manager at a warehouse inspects electronic boxes for defective items. The number of faulty items in each box was recorded and summarized below. If $X$ is the number of faulty items in a box, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Number of Boxes | $13$ | $6$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Faulty Items | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the faulty items, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of boxes is $13+6+4+2=25,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{13}{25} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{6}{25} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{4}{25} \\\\ P(X=3)=f(3) & =\\frac{2}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{13}{25}$ | $\\frac{6}{25}$ | $\\frac{4}{25}$ | $\\frac{2}{25}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =0\\left(\\frac{13}{25}\\right)+1\\left(\\frac{6}{25}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{4}{25}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{2}{25}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{25}+\\frac{8}{25}+\\frac{6}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+8+6}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a977656-5bc0-52c2-8412-7159dca353b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569523} {:answer/id #uuid "4727a762-fdee-5b8f-ab0a-5ac69d4cf5e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569541} {:answer/id #uuid "6aeed908-a161-5da2-adf1-5808f16f101a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569612} {:answer/id #uuid "71bfc73a-a14f-5c8b-a23c-4a17d56fd53e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569630} {:answer/id #uuid "ccbdc6ea-8277-599f-9f2d-8846ff5d253b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccbdc6ea-8277-599f-9f2d-8846ff5d253b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569768} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d1259d7-7153-5c05-8443-bfcd08dbb965" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42a89ddb-b42b-5afd-9218-01f794cbc72e" :question/math-academy-id "q-67501" :question/problem "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,2,\\,…$\n\nII. ${a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3}{2}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=-1,\\,n\\ge 1$\n\nIII. $1,\\,0.2,\\,0.03,\\,…$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not a geometric sequence because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is a geometric sequence. The recursive rule ${a}_{n+1}=\\frac{3}{2}{a}_{n}$ tells us that each term is $\\frac{3}{2}$ times the previous term, so the common ratio is $r=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- Sequence III is not a geometric sequence because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{0.2}{1} & =0.2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{0.03}{0.2} & =0.15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence II is a geometric sequence."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290464f7-629a-599f-9619-f15eb5786d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569498} {:answer/id #uuid "51e0957a-5bf2-56a2-93c7-c85dbc288c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569563} {:answer/id #uuid "5b73964e-ee01-5caf-b596-26a1127dfc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569576} {:answer/id #uuid "9d79ce2e-0688-5dfd-92d7-ae8f08fcc6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569694} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a0885c-49d2-5c12-bd6c-2ba68d197915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b73964e-ee01-5caf-b596-26a1127dfc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569576} :answer-field/id #uuid "850858f3-9f08-5b6d-b191-4d25dc883811" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "522d89ff-41d6-50ec-8d1b-a4ada325fbc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-83981" :question/problem "A stack contains four cards, where each card is labeled $1$, $4$, $6$, or $9.$ If one card is randomly drawn, what is its expected value?" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the label on a randomly drawn card. Then the support is $S=\\{1,4,6,9\\},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\frac{1}{4}:$\n\n$$\nf(1)=f(4)=f(6)=f(9)=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+4\\cdot f(4)+6\\cdot f(6)+9\\cdot f(9) \\\\  & =1\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+4\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+6\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+9\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{4}{4}+\\frac{6}{4}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+4+6+9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{4} \\\\  & =5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569988 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a55a640-5056-53aa-a01c-37549144e131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{43}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569544} {:answer/id #uuid "69532553-7c14-5c9e-8f67-52df69fbcb70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569607} {:answer/id #uuid "a4821310-860e-5fda-9174-3a8a5f122a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{53}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569707} {:answer/id #uuid "ba42e326-790d-5ce1-a2d5-97a6580a41b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{43}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569739} {:answer/id #uuid "ba66511f-5970-5a6f-9b8a-e517905c7f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{43}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba66511f-5970-5a6f-9b8a-e517905c7f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{43}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569741} :answer-field/id #uuid "57adae38-47d4-5119-8e1b-753ad3c2f4c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53e1317a-3ae2-58ac-bb4d-59a740997145" :question/math-academy-id "q-127259" :question/problem "The rolls of an unfair octahedral die follow the probability mass function $f(x)$ as shown in the table below. What is the expected value of one roll of the die?\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{3}{32}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{32}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4)+5\\cdot f(5)+6\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =1\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)+4\\left(\\frac{3}{32}\\right)+5\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)+6\\left(\\frac{1}{32}\\right)+7\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+8\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{2}{16}+\\frac{3}{16}+\\frac{12}{32}+\\frac{5}{8}+\\frac{6}{32}+\\frac{7}{4}+\\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{32}+\\frac{4}{32}+\\frac{6}{32}+\\frac{12}{32}+\\frac{20}{32}+\\frac{6}{32}+\\frac{56}{32}+\\frac{64}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4+6+12+20+6+56+64}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{172}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569459} {:answer/id #uuid "42d752c4-1ced-5442-9d14-2e028d4482e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569535} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0f6439-02a9-5ffc-b967-796d14fd99c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569575} {:answer/id #uuid "a04922be-e134-5966-b10b-e756217fb61e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569698} {:answer/id #uuid "b00a2584-97c8-50c5-81c4-5f4529530f60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569459} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5dc0c374-75b0-5038-a236-6349d3712634" :question/math-academy-id "q-23418" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ is undefined."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee3a7f8d-4e6d-583c-9448-f641c8a25567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee3a7f8d-4e6d-583c-9448-f641c8a25567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569820} :answer-field/id #uuid "eff3eb79-2efb-59dd-8b16-b566385dd9e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f220269-fce2-5308-a544-a882143be2b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-337038" :question/problem "The number of heads facing upward after tossing $3$ unfair coins follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is the expected value of the number of heads facing upward after tossing the $3$ coins? Do not round the answer.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =0\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)+1\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{9}+1 \\\\  & =0+\\frac{3}{9}+\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{9}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+3+2+9}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570000 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce3784e-cee7-5852-894c-71ed7ce007ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569465} {:answer/id #uuid "5c751fdf-2bec-56e1-938d-e60711842729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569578} {:answer/id #uuid "6e02a023-2534-54ea-a95d-0e9b379d35c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569619} {:answer/id #uuid "a90215ce-141d-5583-8efd-be89ae2f0bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569713} {:answer/id #uuid "ede15bcc-7345-509e-8ca3-6f297ed035d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c751fdf-2bec-56e1-938d-e60711842729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569578} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd980177-3d4f-5c6d-a6cb-2c5b9aaa3857" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "700fffbb-cd63-5378-9077-32b4c2add6e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-84067" :question/problem "Several children were asked how many siblings they have. The number of siblings of each child was recorded below. If $X$ is the number of siblings of a randomly selected child, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Number of siblings | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Number of Children | $1$ | $5$ | $3$ | $1$ |" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the number of siblings, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of children is $1+5+3+1=10,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{5}{10}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ P(X=3)=f(3) & =\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =0\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)+1\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{5}{10}+\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+5+6+3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{10} \\\\  & =1.4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53773b9d-b41d-57fa-9993-8ee73ebf788b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569565} {:answer/id #uuid "64bcca0d-1370-5e1e-9384-b9fe6e5c2226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569599} {:answer/id #uuid "74903b10-b9c3-5982-a77c-38ecc70f9cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569636} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba3a52b-1145-5782-88c9-4be78cb79a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569691} {:answer/id #uuid "e4bf7c3d-ba90-54f4-a876-93efb8156cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ba3a52b-1145-5782-88c9-4be78cb79a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569691} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f8080f-0b51-545f-9d71-93e2c857b6d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "857b63a1-09cd-5ba9-9404-4c37877c9864" :question/math-academy-id "q-79511" :question/problem "What is the $4$th term of the geometric sequence given below?\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-6{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{36},\\,n\\ge 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $4$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{36} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =-6{a}_{1}=-6\\left(\\frac{1}{36}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-6{a}_{2}=-6\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\right)=1 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =-6{a}_{3}=-6(1)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${a}_{4}=-6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570012 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e15daab7-6b24-5c89-8928-f0f9fcd1b856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e15daab7-6b24-5c89-8928-f0f9fcd1b856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569803} :answer-field/id #uuid "36c5b657-8ae8-52f1-9b50-9850b824e02a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cdf7c4f-4409-57f0-b1eb-dca5de834f65" :question/math-academy-id "q-276297" :question/problem "A fair spinner has six sides labeled with the numbers $-2,$ $-1,$ $0,$ $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$ If the spinner is spun, what is the expected value of the outcome?\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the outcome of the spinner. Then the support is $S=\\{-2,-1,0,1,2,3\\},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\frac{1}{6}:$\n\n$$\nf(-2)=f(-1)=f(0)=f(1)=f(2)=f(3)=\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =-2\\cdot f(-2)+(-1)\\cdot f(-1)+0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =-2\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+(-1)\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+0\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+1\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{6}-\\frac{1}{6}+0+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{2}{6}+\\frac{3}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-1+0+1+2+3}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "183e7e96-ee7e-5850-a1bf-75f30a215f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{39.81}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569480} {:answer/id #uuid "5a328bb2-83f5-57f5-b38d-f472331c7c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{33.56}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569573} {:answer/id #uuid "6c4ac95c-8556-5fbf-965a-0eb4c8e0345a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{36.44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569616} {:answer/id #uuid "84465da0-5d9b-563a-a817-64751d321a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{44.26}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569661} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7753a4-af15-5a0d-94f5-f6eecf8f7ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{28.18}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a328bb2-83f5-57f5-b38d-f472331c7c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{33.56}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569573} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f77438e-6a65-5147-9492-745b947b2082" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ede5978-9720-5b14-aefb-d172677e353f" :question/math-academy-id "q-14701" :question/problem "![](images/40/40ee0a065e8733bd1ab22c04af39b358da5106791af6b99017cef1d07c13abcd.png)\n\nFor the triangle above, find $m∠A$ in degrees to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use the Law of Cosines to solve for angle $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡A &=\\frac{{b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-{a}^{2}}{2bc}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\na=5,\\,b=8,\\,c=9.\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡A & =\\frac{{9}^{2}+{8}^{2}-{5}^{2}}{2(9)(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{81+64-25}{144} \\\\  & =\\frac{120}{144} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right) \\\\  & ={33.56}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "742dc51a-8d96-560e-94d1-367169dd0392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569634} {:answer/id #uuid "c42187b6-4c29-5b59-b6f3-624ff226be39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569750} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c3238c-1f70-547d-9e2f-c99ec90befda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569784} {:answer/id #uuid "e1476574-57fd-5884-b17f-d8c6e1916591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569802} {:answer/id #uuid "fb85d25b-c824-57bd-a2e9-670821d95958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "742dc51a-8d96-560e-94d1-367169dd0392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569634} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4c2f95-49e5-5e7b-9fbf-f50c23d0825b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a72cce3a-417b-593f-a3ca-475feb70703d" :question/math-academy-id "q-144906" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ is undefined\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{12{sin}^{2}⁡(\\theta ){cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{12{sin}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡(\\theta ){\\cancel{cos}}^{2}(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\cancel{sin}\\theta {cos}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}{0\\cdot (3\\cdot 0-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/32/32a586a1fe160a6c3a2cfee3cfb78af3d596679cf40abedeb0b8d622a3cf736c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}]
